<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-inverse-of-trigonometric-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Inverse of Trigonometric Functions</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Determine the inverse of a function of polynomials and trigonometric ratios
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To help learners discover for themselves why a trigonometric function needs a restricted domain before its inverse can be defined, working from a position on the unit circle back to an angle.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Let learners sketch the unit circle and the right-angled triangle in pairs before any explanation is given. Circulate and ask how many angles could place the point at the same position. Use their answers to lead into the idea of restricting the domain in the structured instruction that follows.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <activity xml:id="activity-exploring-trig-inverses">
        <p>
            Picture a point moving around the unit circle. At a given moment, its position is <m>\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, \frac{1}{2}\right)</m>. Your task is to find the angle <m>x</m> that placed the point there.
        </p>
        <p>
            Sketch a unit circle and mark this point. Then draw a right-angled triangle from the origin to the point, using the horizontal and vertical distances as the two legs.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-activity-unit-circle-point">
            <caption>A point on the unit circle at an unknown angle</caption>
            <image width="45%">
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                        \draw (0,0) circle (1);
                        \draw[-&gt;] (-1.2,0)--(1.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
                        \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.2)--(0,1.2) node[above] {$y$};

                        \filldraw[blue] ({cos(30)},{sin(30)}) circle (1.5pt);
                        \node[above right] at ({cos(30)},{sin(30)}) {$\left(\frac{\sqrt3}{2},\frac12\right)$};

                        \draw[dashed] ({cos(30)},0)--({cos(30)},{sin(30)});
                        \draw[thick, blue] (0,0)--({cos(30)},{sin(30)});
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            Use your sketch to answer the following questions with a partner or as a group.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Using your triangle, find the angle <m>x</m> that gives this position. You may already recognise this as one of the special angles.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Now imagine the point keeps moving all the way around the circle and returns to the exact same position after a full revolution. Write down another angle, larger than <m>2\pi</m>, that also places the point at <m>\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, \frac{1}{2}\right)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Can you find a second angle between <m>0</m> and <m>2\pi</m> where the vertical coordinate is also <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, even though the horizontal coordinate is different? Mark this second point on your sketch.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Based on what you have found, if someone tells you only that <m>\sin(x) = \frac{1}{2}</m>, can you be certain which single angle they mean? What would need to change for there to be only one possible answer?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    20 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise the idea of a restricted domain and to introduce the definitions, domains, and ranges of the three inverse trigonometric functions.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Begin by asking learners to share what they noticed in the activity, particularly the fact that more than one angle can give the same sine value. Use this to explain why we must restrict the domain before the inverse can exist.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Reading the Table:</alert> Walk learners through the domain and range table for sine, cosine, and tangent. Stress that the domain of the inverse function is the range of the original restricted function, and that the range of the inverse function is the restricted domain of the original.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>A Common Misconception:</alert> Make sure learners understand that <m>\sin^{-1}x</m> is not the same as <m>\frac{1}{\sin x}</m>. The notation looks similar, but the inverse sine returns an angle, while the reciprocal returns a number.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>
    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
            <p>
                Inverse trigonometric functions reverse the action of the trigonometric functions. For example, if <m>y=\sin^{-1}x</m>, then <m>x=\sin y</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The sine, cosine, and tangent functions are not one-to-one on their full domains, so their domains must be restricted before inverses can be defined.
            </p>
            <p>
                The standard restricted domains are chosen so that each function becomes one-to-one and includes the angle <m>0</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The inverse trigonometric functions are defined in the table below.
            </p>
            <table>
                <title>Inverse Trigonometric Functions</title>
                <tabular>
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>Function</cell>
                        <cell>Domain</cell>
                        <cell>Range</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>y=\sin^{-1}x</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>-1 \le x \le 1</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>-\frac{\pi}{2} \le y \le \frac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>y=\cos^{-1}x</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>-1 \le x \le 1</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0 \le y \le \pi</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>y=\tan^{-1}x</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>-\infty &lt; x &lt; \infty</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>-\frac{\pi}{2} &lt; y &lt; \frac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                Note that <m>\sin^{-1}x</m> does not mean <m>\frac{1}{\sin x}</m>. The output of an inverse trigonometric function is an angle, usually measured in radians.
            </p>
            <p>
                The graph of an inverse trigonometric function is the reflection of the original graph in the line <m>y=x</m>.
            </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-arcsine-graph">
            <caption>Sine and inverse sine</caption>

            <image width="55%" xml:id="img-arcsine-reflection">
                <shortdescription>
                    A graph showing the restricted sine function and the inverse sine function reflected across the line y = x.
                </shortdescription>

                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            width=8cm,
                            height=8cm,
                            axis equal image,
                            axis lines=middle,
                            xmin=-2, xmax=2,
                            ymin=-2, ymax=2,
                            xlabel={$x$},
                            ylabel={$y$},
                            xtick={-1,1},
                            ytick={-1.5708,-1,1,1.5708},
                            yticklabels={$-\frac{\pi}{2}$,$-1$,$1$,$\frac{\pi}{2}$},
                            legend pos=south east,
                            legend style={font=\scriptsize, draw=none, fill=white},
                            samples=150,
                            clip=false
                        ]
                            \addplot[dashed, red, thick, domain=-1.8:1.8] {x};
                            \addlegendentry{$y=x$}

                            \addplot[blue, ultra thick, domain=-1.5708:1.5708] {sin(deg(x))};
                            \addlegendentry{$y=\sin x$}

                            \addplot[teal, ultra thick, domain=-1:1] {rad(asin(x))};
                            \addlegendentry{$y=\sin^{-1}x$}
                        \end{axis}
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-arccosine-graph">
            <caption>Cosine and inverse cosine</caption>

            <image width="55%" xml:id="img-arccosine-reflection">
                <shortdescription>
                    A graph showing the restricted cosine function and the inverse cosine function reflected across the line y = x.
                </shortdescription>

                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            width=8cm,
                            height=8cm,
                            axis equal image,
                            axis lines=middle,
                            xmin=-1.4, xmax=3.4,
                            ymin=-1.4, ymax=3.4,
                            xlabel={$x$},
                            ylabel={$y$},
                            xtick={-1,1,3.1416},
                            xticklabels={$-1$,$1$,$\pi$},
                            ytick={-1,1,3.1416},
                            yticklabels={$-1$,$1$,$\pi$},
                            legend pos=north east,
                            legend style={font=\scriptsize, draw=none, fill=white},
                            samples=150,
                            clip=false
                        ]
                            \addplot[dashed, red, thick, domain=-1.2:3.2] {x};
                            \addlegendentry{$y=x$}

                            \addplot[blue, ultra thick, domain=0:3.1416] {cos(deg(x))};
                            \addlegendentry{$y=\cos x$}

                            \addplot[teal, ultra thick, domain=-1:1] {rad(acos(x))};
                            \addlegendentry{$y=\cos^{-1}x$}
                        \end{axis}
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-arctangent-graph">
            <caption>Tangent and inverse tangent</caption>

            <image width="55%" xml:id="img-arctangent-reflection">
                <shortdescription>
                    A graph showing the restricted tangent function and the inverse tangent function reflected across the line y = x.
                </shortdescription>

                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            width=8cm,
                            height=8cm,
                            axis equal image,
                            axis lines=middle,
                            xmin=-3, xmax=3,
                            ymin=-3, ymax=3,
                            xlabel={$x$},
                            ylabel={$y$},
                            xtick={-1.5708,-1,1,1.5708},
                            xticklabels={$-\frac{\pi}{2}$,$-1$,$1$,$\frac{\pi}{2}$},
                            ytick={-1.5708,-1,1,1.5708},
                            yticklabels={$-\frac{\pi}{2}$,$-1$,$1$,$\frac{\pi}{2}$},
                            legend pos=south east,
                            legend style={font=\scriptsize, draw=none, fill=white},
                            samples=180,
                            clip=false
                        ]
                            \addplot[dashed, red, thick, domain=-2.8:2.8] {x};
                            \addlegendentry{$y=x$}

                            \addplot[blue, ultra thick, domain=-1.25:1.25] {tan(deg(x))};
                            \addlegendentry{$y=\tan x$}

                            \addplot[teal, ultra thick, domain=-3:3] {rad(atan(x))};
                            \addlegendentry{$y=\tan^{-1}x$}
                        \end{axis}
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </insight>

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example xml:id="ex-eval-inv-sine">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate <m>\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We need to determine the angle whose sine value is <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Recall that inverse sine only gives angles in the interval
                <m>\left[-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2}\right]</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                From the special angles,
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)=\frac{1}{2}</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m> lies in the required interval, it is the principal value.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore,
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)=\frac{\pi}{6}</md>
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-inv-sine-half">
                <caption>Angle whose sine is one-half</caption>
                <image width="45%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                            \draw (0,0) circle (1);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (-1.2,0)--(1.2,0);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.2)--(0,1.2);

                            \draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--({cos(30)},{sin(30)});
                            \draw[dashed] ({cos(30)},0)--({cos(30)},{sin(30)});

                            \node[right] at ({cos(30)},{sin(30)}) {$\left(\frac{\sqrt3}{2},\frac12\right)$};

                            \node at (0.3,0.15) {$\frac{\pi}{6}$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-eval-inv-cosine">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the exact value of <m>\cos^{-1}(-1)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Here we are searching for an angle whose cosine is <m>-1</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                The output of inverse cosine must belong to the interval <m>[0,\pi]</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                On the unit circle, the cosine value corresponds to the horizontal coordinate.
            </p>

            <p>
                The point with horizontal coordinate <m>-1</m> is <m>(-1,0)</m>, which occurs at the angle <m>\pi</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Hence,
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>\cos^{-1}(-1)=\pi</md>
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-arccos-negative-one">
                <caption>Angle whose cosine is negative one</caption>

                <image width="45%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                            \draw (0,0) circle (1);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (-1.2,0)--(1.2,0);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.2)--(0,1.2);

                            \filldraw[blue] (-1,0) circle (1.5pt);

                            \draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(-1,0);

                            \node[above left] at (-1,0) {$(-1,0)$};

                            \node at (-0.35,0.15) {$\pi$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-eval-inv-tangent">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate <m>\tan^{-1}(1)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We know that tangent is defined as
                <m>\tan\theta=\frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{adjacent}}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                A tangent value of <m>1</m> means the opposite and adjacent sides are equal.
            </p>
            <p>
                This happens at the angle
                <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Since inverse tangent returns angles in
                <m>\left(-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m>,
                the angle <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m> is valid.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore,
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>\tan^{-1}(1)=\frac{\pi}{4}</md>
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-arctan-one">
                <caption>Angle whose tangent is one</caption>
                <image width="45%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                            \draw[-&gt;] (-0.2,0)--(1.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-0.2)--(0,1.3) node[above] {$y$};

                            \draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(1,1);
                            \draw[dashed] (1,0)--(1,1);

                            \node[right] at (1,1) {$(1,1)$};

                            \node at (0.35,0.15) {$\frac{\pi}{4}$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-eval-arcsine-negative">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the exact value of <m>\sin^{-1}\left(-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                First identify the reference angle associated with
                <m>\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Since
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                the reference angle is
                <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Because the sine value is negative, the required angle must be below the <m>x</m>-axis.
            </p>
            <p>
                Within the range of inverse sine,
                <m>\left[-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2}\right]</m>,
                the correct angle is
                <m>-\frac{\pi}{4}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore,
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>\sin^{-1}\left(-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)=-\frac{\pi}{4}</md>
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-arcsin-negative-root2">
                <caption>Negative angle for inverse sine</caption>
                <image width="45%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                            \draw (0,0) circle (1);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (-1.2,0)--(1.2,0);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.2)--(0,1.2);

                            \draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--({cos(-45)},{sin(-45)});

                            \node[right] at ({cos(-45)},{sin(-45)})
                            {$\left(\frac{\sqrt2}{2},-\frac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)$};

                            \node at (0.35,-0.18) {$-\frac{\pi}{4}$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-eval-arccosine-zero">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate <m>\cos^{-1}(0)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To evaluate this expression, locate where cosine equals zero on the unit circle.
            </p>
            <p>
                Cosine represents the horizontal coordinate of a point on the circle.
            </p>
            <p>
                The horizontal coordinate becomes zero at the top of the circle.
            </p>
            <p>
                That position corresponds to the angle
                <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Since this angle lies within the inverse cosine range <m>[0,\pi]</m>, it is the required value.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore,
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>\cos^{-1}(0)=\frac{\pi}{2}</md>
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-arccos-zero">
                <caption>Angle whose cosine is zero</caption>
                <image width="45%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                            \draw (0,0) circle (1);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (-1.2,0)--(1.2,0);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.2)--(0,1.2);

                            \draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(0,1);

                            \filldraw[blue] (0,1) circle (1.5pt);

                            \node[left] at (0,1) {$(0,1)$};

                            \node at (0.18,0.35) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-inverse-trig">
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate each inverse trigonometric expression to find its exact value. If an expression is undefined, explain why.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><m>\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m></li>
                        <li><m>\cos^{-1}\left(-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)</m></li>
                        <li><m>\tan^{-1}(\sqrt{3})</m></li>
                        <li><m>\sin^{-1}(2)</m></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><p><m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\frac{3\pi}{4}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Undefined</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We are looking for an angle <m>\theta</m> in the interval <m>[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]</m> such that <m>\sin(\theta) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>. This angle is <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We seek an angle <m>\theta</m> in the interval <m>[0, \pi]</m> where <m>\cos(\theta) = -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>. Because the cosine is negative, the angle is in Quadrant II. The reference angle is <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m>, so the angle is <m>\pi - \frac{\pi}{4} = \frac{3\pi}{4}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We need an angle <m>\theta</m> in the interval <m>(-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2})</m> such that <m>\tan(\theta) = \sqrt{3}</m>. This occurs at <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The domain of the inverse sine function is <m>[-1, 1]</m>. Because <m>2</m> is outside of this domain, the expression <m>\sin^{-1}(2)</m> is undefined. There is no real angle whose sine is <m>2</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compare the exact values of the expressions below without the aid of a calculator. State which value is greater and provide a clear mathematical justification for your conclusion.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                    \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)
                    \quad\text{or}\quad
                    \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)</m> is greater.
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    To compare the two, we evaluate the exact angle for each expression:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p><m>\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) = \frac{\pi}{6}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) = \frac{\pi}{3}</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Since <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m> is equivalent to <m>\frac{2\pi}{6}</m>, it is clear that <m>\frac{\pi}{3} &gt; \frac{\pi}{6}</m>. Therefore, <m>\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)</m> is the greater value.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the exact value of <m>x</m> that satisfies the following equation:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        \sin^{-1}(x) = \frac{\pi}{6}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>x = \frac{1}{2}</m></p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    To isolate <m>x</m>, we apply the sine function to both sides of the equation. Since <m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m> is within the restricted range of the inverse sine function, we can safely apply this operation:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\sin(\sin^{-1}(x)) \amp = \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)</mrow>
                        <mrow>x \amp = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write the composite trigonometric expression below as an algebraic expression in terms of <m>x</m>. Assume that <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        \sin(\cos^{-1}(x))
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>\sqrt{1-x^2}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let <m>\theta = \cos^{-1}(x)</m>. By the definition of the inverse cosine function, this implies that <m>\cos(\theta) = x</m>. Because we are assuming <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, <m>\theta</m> lies in the first quadrant.
                </p>
                <p>
                    We can visualize this relationship by drawing a right triangle where the adjacent side has a length of <m>x</m> and the hypotenuse has a length of <m>1</m>.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-right-triangle-cos-inv">
                    <caption>Right triangle model for <m>\cos(\theta) = x</m></caption>
                    <image width="40%" xml:id="img-right-triangle-cos-inv">
                        <shortdescription>A right triangle with an angle theta, adjacent side of length x, hypotenuse of length 1, and an opposite side of length square root of (1 minus x squared).</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5]
                                \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (3,0) node[midway, below] {$x$} -- (3,2) node[midway, right] {$\sqrt{1-x^2}$} -- (0,0) node[midway, above left] {$1$};
                                \draw (2.8,0) -- (2.8,0.2) -- (3,0.2);
                                \draw (0.5,0) arc (0:33.7:0.5);
                                \node at (0.7,0.2) {$\theta$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    Using the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the opposite side is <m>\sqrt{1^2 - x^2} = \sqrt{1-x^2}</m>. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    We are asked to find <m>\sin(\theta)</m>, which is the ratio of the opposite side to the hypotenuse:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                    \sin(\theta) = \frac{\sqrt{1-x^2}}{1} = \sqrt{1-x^2}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The figure below shows the graph of the restricted sine function, <m>y = \sin(x)</m>, and its inverse, <m>y = \sin^{-1}(x)</m>.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-sine-arcsine-reflection">
                    <caption>Graphs of <m>y = \sin(x)</m> and <m>y = \sin^{-1}(x)</m></caption>
                    <image width="50%" xml:id="img-sine-arcsine">
                        <shortdescription>Graphs of the restricted sine function and arcsine function showing reflection across the dashed line y=x.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
                                \draw[-&gt;] (-2,0) -- (2,0) node[right]{$x$};
                                \draw[-&gt;] (0,-2) -- (0,2) node[above]{$y$};
                                \draw[dashed, gray] (-2,-2) -- (2,2) node[right]{$y=x$};
                                \draw[domain=-1.57:1.57,smooth,thick,blue] plot (\x,{sin(\x r)}) node[right]{$y=\sin(x)$};
                                \draw[domain=-1:1,smooth,thick,red] plot (\x,{asin(\x)*pi/180}) node[above left]{$y=\sin^{-1}(x)$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    Explain the geometric relationship between these two curves. Why do they appear as reflections of one another across the line <m>y = x</m>? Additionally, state the restricted domain and range for both functions.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    The coordinates <m>(x, y)</m> of the original function become <m>(y, x)</m> on the inverse function, which geometrically causes a reflection across the line <m>y = x</m>. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>y = \sin(x)</m> Domain: <m>[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]</m>, Range: <m>[-1, 1]</m>.
                    <m>y = \sin^{-1}(x)</m> Domain: <m>[-1, 1]</m>, Range: <m>[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    By definition, an inverse function is created by interchanging the input (<m>x</m>) and output (<m>y</m>) variables. Because every point <m>(a, b)</m> on the sine curve maps to a corresponding point <m>(b, a)</m> on the arcsine curve, the resulting graph is an exact mirror reflection across the diagonal line <m>y = x</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Because the variables swap, the domain and range also swap:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>For <m>y = \sin(x)</m>, the restricted domain is <m>[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]</m> and the range is <m>[-1, 1]</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>For <m>y = \sin^{-1}(x)</m>, the domain is <m>[-1, 1]</m> and the range is <m>[-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}]</m>.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Analyze the inverse cosine function, <m>y = \cos^{-1}(x)</m>. Sketch its graph and determine the following key features:
                </p>

                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>The domain</li>
                    <li>The range</li>
                    <li>The coordinates of the <m>x</m>-intercept</li>
                    <li>Whether the function is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Domain: <m>[-1,1]</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Range: <m>[0,\pi]</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>x</m>-intercept: <m>(1,0)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Strictly decreasing</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Below is the sketch of the function <m>y = \cos^{-1}(x)</m>.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-arccosine-graph-ex">
                    <caption>Graph of <m>y = \cos^{-1}(x)</m></caption>
                    <image width="50%" xml:id="img-arccosine-graph-ex">
                        <shortdescription>Graph of the inverse cosine function, y = arccos(x), starting at (-1, pi), crossing the y-axis at (0, pi/2), and ending at the x-intercept (1, 0).</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5]
                                \draw[-&gt;] (-1.5,0) -- (1.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
                                \draw[-&gt;] (0,-0.5) -- (0,3.5) node[above]{$y$};
                                \draw[domain=-1:1,smooth,thick,blue] plot (\x,{acos(\x)*pi/180});
                                \fill (-1, {pi}) circle (1.5pt) node[above left]{$(-1, \pi)$};
                                \fill (0, {pi/2}) circle (1.5pt) node[below left]{$(0, \frac{\pi}{2})$};
                                \fill (1, 0) circle (1.5pt) node[above right]{$(1, 0)$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The domain of the inverse function is the range of the restricted cosine function: <m>[-1, 1]</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The range of the inverse function is the restricted domain of the cosine function: <m>[0, \pi]</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The <m>x</m>-intercept occurs when <m>y = 0</m>. Since <m>\cos(0) = 1</m>, the <m>x</m>-intercept is at the point <m>(1, 0)</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                As seen in the graph, as the <m>x</m>-values increase from <m>-1</m> to <m>1</m>, the <m>y</m>-values decrease steadily from <m>\pi</m> to <m>0</m>. Therefore, the function is strictly decreasing.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Investigate the end behavior of the inverse tangent function, <m>y = \tan^{-1}(x)</m>. Predict what happens to the output values of <m>y</m> as <m>x \to \infty</m> and as <m>x \to -\infty</m>. Support your explanation using a sketch of the graph.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    As <m>x \to \infty</m>, <m>y \to \frac{\pi}{2}</m>. As <m>x \to -\infty</m>, <m>y \to -\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The original tangent function has vertical asymptotes at <m>x = \frac{\pi}{2}</m> and <m>x = -\frac{\pi}{2}</m> on its restricted domain. Because inverse functions swap the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> variables, the vertical asymptotes of the tangent function become horizontal asymptotes for the inverse tangent function.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-arctangent-end-behavior">
                    <caption>Graph of <m>y = \tan^{-1}(x)</m> showing horizontal asymptotes</caption>
                    <image width="55%" xml:id="img-arctangent-end-behavior">
                        <shortdescription>Graph of the inverse tangent function approaching horizontal asymptotes at y = pi/2 as x goes to infinity, and y = -pi/2 as x goes to negative infinity.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
                                \draw[-&gt;] (-4.5,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right]{$x$};
                                \draw[-&gt;] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above]{$y$};
                                \draw[dashed, thick, gray] (-4.5, {pi/2}) -- (4.5, {pi/2}) node[right, black]{$y=\frac{\pi}{2}$};
                                \draw[dashed, thick, gray] (-4.5, {-pi/2}) -- (4.5, {-pi/2}) node[right, black]{$y=-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
                                \draw[domain=-4.2:4.2,smooth,thick,blue] plot (\x,{atan(\x)*pi/180});
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    Observing the graph, we can see the end behavior:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>As the input <m>x</m> grows infinitely large in the positive direction (<m>x \to \infty</m>), the curve flattens out and approaches the horizontal line <m>y = \frac{\pi}{2}</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>As the input <m>x</m> grows infinitely large in the negative direction (<m>x \to -\infty</m>), the curve approaches the horizontal line <m>y = -\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Critique the following mathematical claim made by a student:
                </p>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>"For all real numbers <m>x</m>, the equation <m>\cos^{-1}(\cos(x)) = x</m> is always true."</p>
                </blockquote>
                <p>
                    Determine if this statement is valid. If it is false, provide a theoretical justification and a counterexample to disprove it.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    The statement is false. The identity is only true when <m>x</m> is in the restricted domain <m>[0, \pi]</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The claim is false. While it is generally true that an inverse function "undoes" the action of the original function, trigonometric functions are periodic. To create the inverse cosine function, we had to strictly restrict the domain of the original cosine function to the interval <m>[0, \pi]</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Because the output (range) of <m>\cos^{-1}(x)</m> is explicitly limited to <m>[0, \pi]</m>, it is impossible for the expression <m>\cos^{-1}(\cos(x))</m> to return an angle outside of this interval, even if you input an angle outside of it.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <alert>Counterexample:</alert> Let <m>x = 2\pi</m>. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\cos^{-1}(\cos(2\pi)) \amp = \cos^{-1}(1)</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 0</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    As shown, plugging in <m>x = 2\pi</m> results in an output of <m>0</m>, not <m>2\pi</m>. Therefore, <m>\cos^{-1}(\cos(x)) = x</m> only holds true if <m>0 \le x \le \pi</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>

        <table>
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Give learners a printed copy of the domain and range table from the insight block to keep beside them during practice. Start with only the inverse sine examples, since the small interval makes the principal value easier to identify, before moving on to inverse cosine and inverse tangent. Encourage learners to sketch the relevant quarter or half of the unit circle for each question rather than working from memory.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Challenge learners to find and prove a similar counterexample for <m>\sin^{-1}(\sin(x))</m>, using an angle outside the restricted domain of sine. Learners can also investigate composite expressions such as <m>\cos(\tan^{-1}(x))</m> using a right triangle model, similar to the method used in the exercises, and present their reasoning to the class.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

    <exploration xml:id="explore-inverse-trigonometric-functions">
        <title>Which angle gives this ratio?</title>
        <p>
            Choose <m>\sin^{-1}</m>, <m>\cos^{-1}</m> or <m>\tan^{-1}</m>, then move the ratio slider. The graph of the selected inverse function is drawn and a point marks the angle produced by the current ratio, with the ratio, the angle and the inverse expression shown alongside.
        </p>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-inverse-trigonometric-functions" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/functions/inverse-trigonometric-functions.js">
            <sbsgroup>
                <sidebyside width="95%" margins="2%">
                    <slate xml:id="jsx-inverse-trigonometric-functions" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
                </sidebyside>
                <sidebyside width="95%" margins="2%">
                    <slate surface="html">
                        <div style="text-align:center;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
                            <input type="button" value="sin⁻¹" onclick="itSelect(0);" onkeypress="itSelect(0);" style="margin:3px;font-size:14px;padding:3px 12px;"/>
                            <input type="button" value="cos⁻¹" onclick="itSelect(1);" onkeypress="itSelect(1);" style="margin:3px;font-size:14px;padding:3px 12px;"/>
                            <input type="button" value="tan⁻¹" onclick="itSelect(2);" onkeypress="itSelect(2);" style="margin:3px;font-size:14px;padding:3px 12px;"/>
                        </div>
                    </slate>
                </sidebyside>
            </sbsgroup>
        </interactive>
    </exploration>

